<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rimush</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2279–2270 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/23rd-century-bce">23rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rimush was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> and a son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a>. His reign was dominated by campaigns to suppress revolts among the Sumerian cities and other subject territories. Both Rimush and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manishtushu">Manishtushu</a> were assassinated, illustrating the empire's dependence on the personal military ability of its rulers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rimush.jpg" alt="Rimush" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zunkir · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jar_fragment_Rimush_Tell_Brak_127340.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rimush, also written Rimuš, was the second king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> and a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a>. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manishtushu">Manishtushu</a> succeeded him, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> was his nephew. His sister was Enheduana, considered the earliest known named author in world history. Naram-Sin deified Sargon and Manishtushu after their deaths but not his uncle. The city of Dur-Rimuš, the Fortress of Rimush, stood near Tell Ishchali and Khafajah and was a cult centre of the storm god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> his reign lasted nine years, though variant copies read seven or fifteen. Most of it went to consolidating the empire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a> had made, which reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> in the east, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the north and the lower sea in the south. His inscriptions record widespread revolts, and he had to reconquer the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a>, Adab, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a>, Der, KI.AN and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazallu">Kazallu</a> from rebellious governors.</p><p>One inscription claims that at Adab and Zabalam he struck down 15,718 men and took 14,576 captives, and another that at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> and KI.AN he struck down 8,900 and took 3,540. These accounts say he destroyed city walls and expelled and annihilated thousands more, and they speak of enormous Sumerian losses.</p><p>He also campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and Marhashi. After the conquest of Elam he dedicated 30 mana of gold, 3,600 mana of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and 360 slaves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manishtushu">Manishtushu</a>, Elam was ruled by Akkadian military governors. Votive offerings of Rimush have been found in temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>, Khafajah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brak">Brak</a>, and an inscription records a statue of himself that he placed before Enlil at Nippur; its metal is variously read as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, bronze or meteoric iron. A victory stele in fragments from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a>, attributed to him on stylistic grounds although some propose the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a>, shows Akkadian soldiers slaughtering prisoners and records land grants taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> for the Akkadian nobility.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tradition holds that Rimush was assassinated. A first-millennium BCE compendium of omens, the Bārûtu, records an omen of King Rimush, whom his courtiers killed with their cylinder seals. He was succeeded by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manishtushu">Manishtushu</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rimush” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The revolts of Sargon's later reign and of the reigns of <b>Rimush</b> and Naram-Sin suggest that this accommodation was fragile and that imperial demands often exceeded what subject cities considered legitimate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Rimush</b> reconquered the rebellious cities with mass slaughter, recording for Ur and Lagash 8,049 killed, 5,460 captured and enslaved and 5,985 expelled and annihilated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Rimush</b> had to put down a revolt led by a local ruler of Ur; an inscription records 8,040 men killed, 5,460 taken prisoner and the leaders of the rebellion captured, and Rimush dedicated vessels seized in his Elamite campaign to the gods at Ur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings of Awan led a coalition of Elamite lands and resisted the Akkadian Empire: Sargon and <b>Rimush</b> record victories over Elam and its allies.</blockquote><a href="/state/awan-dynasty">Awan dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Its kings allied with Elam against Sargon and <b>Rimush</b> of the Akkadian Empire, and Rimush records defeating Abalgamash, king of Marhashi.</blockquote><a href="/state/marhashi">Marhashi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Akkad. Died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
